<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.auth-timeseries — MASTER M6 (backlog b22e519e06cb), DoD 2. Every 5 min: sample each
  account's keychain credential state into a durable append store.

  WHY A JOB AT ALL. tools/auth/auth-timeseries.sh has been on trunk since 2ec33a27 and it is a
  good instrument — it is the only thing that can see a refresh-token ROTATION or the moment a
  credential goes EMPTY, which is what a forced logout looks like from outside. But it was
  MANUAL and TIME-BOUNDED: a 6-hour ad-hoc run to a caller-supplied path. Nothing scheduled it,
  no durable per-account store accumulated, and so every observation died with the session that
  made it. The forced-logout investigation could only ever be run in retrospect, on an event
  that had already left no trace. This is the thing that fires it.

  WHY --once AND NOT KeepAlive. The script loops internally for its whole duration. Under
  StartInterval the unmodified script would start a fresh 6-hour process every tick and pile
  them up. --once makes ONE sample batch the unit of work and lets launchd own the cadence,
  which is also what makes the cadence auditable from this file instead of from an argument
  somebody typed once.

  READ-ONLY, PROVEN BY READING IT. Per tick: 12 `/usr/bin/security find-generic-password` reads
  (6 rows x 2 — one attribute dump for the write time, one -w for the payload) and one
  `ps -wwEo command=` sweep. NO network call of any kind. NO `security add`/`-U`/`delete`, no
  token refresh, no `claude` invocation, no config-dir write. The ONLY mutation in the entire
  script is the `>>` append to its own store. It never emits a token value — only sha256[:12]
  prefixes, so a rotation is visible without the secret being.

  🚨 THE KEYCHAIN-ACL CAVEAT, AND WHY IT IS NAMED HERE RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER.
  `security find-generic-password -w` is subject to the item's ACL. Under launchd there is no
  interactive session to answer an "allow access" dialog, so if /usr/bin/security is not already
  trusted for these items the call fails — and the script's deliberate `2>/dev/null` (one
  unreadable item must not abort the batch) would render that denial as a clean run of NO_ITEM
  rows, i.e. a FALSE "the credential is gone" on every account at once. That is the worst
  possible failure for this particular instrument, because NO_ITEM is the exact signal it exists
  to detect. Two defences: (1) the operator activation script hand-runs one --once batch from a
  non-tty context BEFORE arming, and refuses to arm on anything but OK rows; (2) the script now
  exits 3 (NO-DATA) when a --once batch appends ZERO rows, so a total ACL denial is a non-zero
  exit rather than a silent success. A partial denial still needs (1) — hence the gate.
  (Non-zero here is a DESIGNED verdict, per daemon-fleet-v2: do not "fix" it to always exit 0.)

  COST AND GROWTH. 6 rows/tick at ~300 B. At StartInterval 300 that is ~0.5 MB/day, reaching the
  25 MiB rotation threshold in ~50 days. 300 s rather than the investigation's 60 s deliberately:
  the question this store answers is "when did the credential change", and mdat (the keychain
  item's own write time) is recorded on every row, so the SAMPLE rate bounds detection latency
  while the WRITE time stays exact regardless. 60 s would buy 5x the rows for no extra precision
  on the fact that matters. Registered in scripts/rotate-autonomy-logs.sh DEFAULT_TARGETS and in
  config/store-bounds.manifest, both — the two rotation mechanisms are independent and a store
  wants both.

  IT NEVER REFUSES ANYTHING — it records and exits. Verdicts: 0 sampled / 3 NO-DATA.

  🚨 THE SUBJECT IS NOT IN THE LIVE LAYER YET, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE — READ BEFORE ARMING.
  The script's home is tools/auth/auth-timeseries.sh, and `tools/` is NOT deployed: there is no
  ~/.claude/tools, and ~/.claude/scripts/auth-timeseries.sh does not exist today. The live layer
  is reached by PER-FILE symlinks, so a file that is merely ADDED to the repo has no link and is
  absent from every path the box can reach — an `exec` against it fails, and a `[ -f x ] &&`
  guard elsewhere would skip it silently. The ProgramArguments path above is therefore a
  PROMISE the activation script keeps: 35-auth-timeseries-activate.sh creates that symlink (and
  refuses to arm if it cannot), which is the same shape 23-qos-census-activate.sh uses. Do not
  bootstrap this label by hand without creating the link first.

  C10: the OPERATOR loads it. Declared `staged` in launchd/fleet.manifest, so install.sh will
  copy the plist and deliberately NOT activate it; migrations/0008-auth-timeseries-activation.sh
  files the operator step. Kill switches after load: `launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.claude.auth-timeseries`,
  or AUTH_TS_OUT to redirect the store.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.auth-timeseries</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; exec "$HOME/.claude/scripts/auth-timeseries.sh" --once</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>300</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>ProcessType</key>      <string>Background</string>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>    <true/>
    <key>Nice</key>             <integer>10</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>  <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/auth-timeseries.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/auth-timeseries.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
